---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { frontendSeries } from '../../data/series';
const inlineStyle = `
.fe17-hero{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#e17055 0%,#fdcb6e 100%)}
.fe17-num{color:#e17055;background:#fdf2ef}

/* ── Hero Art: Utility class chips ── */
.tw-art{margin:2.5rem auto 0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:0.75rem;animation:fadeInUp 1.5s ease}
.tw-chips{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.375rem;justify-content:center;max-width:18rem}
.tw-chip{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.6rem;font-weight:700;padding:0.3rem 0.625rem;border-radius:0.375rem;animation:chipPop 0.4s ease both}
.tc1{background:rgba(116,185,255,0.4);color:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(116,185,255,0.7);animation-delay:0.1s}
.tc2{background:rgba(85,239,196,0.4);color:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(85,239,196,0.7);animation-delay:0.2s}
.tc3{background:rgba(253,203,110,0.4);color:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(253,203,110,0.7);animation-delay:0.3s}
.tc4{background:rgba(225,112,85,0.4);color:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(225,112,85,0.7);animation-delay:0.4s}
.tc5{background:rgba(162,155,254,0.4);color:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(162,155,254,0.7);animation-delay:0.5s}
.tc6{background:rgba(116,185,255,0.4);color:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(116,185,255,0.7);animation-delay:0.6s}
.tc7{background:rgba(85,239,196,0.4);color:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(85,239,196,0.7);animation-delay:0.7s}
.tc8{background:rgba(253,203,110,0.4);color:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(253,203,110,0.7);animation-delay:0.8s}
.tc9{background:rgba(225,112,85,0.4);color:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(225,112,85,0.7);animation-delay:0.9s}
.tc10{background:rgba(162,155,254,0.4);color:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(162,155,254,0.7);animation-delay:1s}
@keyframes chipPop{from{opacity:0;transform:scale(0.7)}to{opacity:1;transform:scale(1)}}
.tw-label{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.65rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.65)}

/* ── CSS problem cards ── */
.problem-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(13rem,1fr));gap:0.75rem;max-width:44rem;margin:1.5rem auto;padding:0 1.5rem}
.problem-card{background:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid #f0f0f0;border-radius:0.75rem;padding:1rem 1.25rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055}
.problem-card strong{color:#2d3436;display:block;margin-bottom:0.25rem}
.problem-card p{font-size:0.85rem;color:#636e72;margin:0}

/* ── Class reference ── */
.class-ref{max-width:40rem;margin:1.5rem auto;padding:0 1.5rem}
.cr-box{background:#1e272e;border-radius:1rem;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border:0.0625rem solid #485460}
.cr-title{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.7rem;font-weight:700;color:#b2bec3;letter-spacing:0.125rem;margin-bottom:1rem}
.cr-row{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:0.75rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap}
.cr-class{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.75rem;font-weight:700;color:#55efc4;min-width:9rem}
.cr-arrow{color:#636e72;font-size:0.8rem}
.cr-css{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.7rem;color:#fdcb6e}

/* ── Quiz theming ep17 ── */
.quiz-ep17 .quiz-q-num{color:#e17055;background:#fdf2ef}
.quiz-ep17 .quiz-reveal-btn{color:#e17055}
.quiz-ep17 .quiz-opt-letter{color:#e17055}
.quiz-ep17 .quiz-option:hover{border-color:#e17055;background:#fdf2ef}
.quiz-ep17 .quiz-answer-highlight{background:#fdf2ef;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055}
`;
const bodyHtml = `

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero fe17-hero">
  <div class="hero-badge">Episode 18 of 23</div>
  <h1>Tailwind CSS</h1>
  <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.95)">Stop writing CSS files. Apply pre-built utility classes directly in your HTML. Every class does exactly one thing.</p>
  <div class="tw-art">
    <div class="tw-chips">
      <span class="tw-chip tc1">tw-flex</span>
      <span class="tw-chip tc2">tw-gap-4</span>
      <span class="tw-chip tc3">tw-px-8</span>
      <span class="tw-chip tc4">tw-mt-8</span>
      <span class="tw-chip tc5">tw-text-white</span>
      <span class="tw-chip tc6">tw-flex-col</span>
      <span class="tw-chip tc7">tw-rounded</span>
      <span class="tw-chip tc8">tw-justify-between</span>
      <span class="tw-chip tc9">tw-border-t-4</span>
      <span class="tw-chip tc10">tw-items-center</span>
    </div>
    <span class="tw-label">pre-written. one job each. apply directly in HTML.</span>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">01</span>
  <h2>The Problems With Regular CSS</h2>
  <p>Before understanding Tailwind, you need to understand what it is solving. Writing CSS manually has four specific problems that get worse as a project grows:</p>
</section>

<div class="problem-grid reveal">
  <div class="problem-card">
    <strong>Naming is exhausting</strong>
    <p>.submit-button-wrapper-inner — what is that? You spend real time inventing class names for every element.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="problem-card">
    <strong>CSS grows forever</strong>
    <p>You add styles, you never delete them. Scared to remove anything in case it breaks something else. The file balloons.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="problem-card">
    <strong>Inconsistency creeps in</strong>
    <p>Dev 1 uses padding: 10px. Dev 2 uses padding: 12px. Dev 3 uses padding: 9px. Everything looks slightly off.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="problem-card">
    <strong>Constant file switching</strong>
    <p>Write HTML → switch to CSS file → add style → switch back → repeat 100 times a day. Breaks your flow.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">02</span>
  <h2>What Tailwind Is</h2>
  <p>Tailwind is a CSS framework that gives you thousands of <span class="highlight">pre-written utility classes</span>. Instead of writing CSS yourself and applying it via class names you invented, you apply Tailwind's pre-made classes directly in your HTML.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">&lt;!-- Without Tailwind: invent a name, write CSS separately --&gt;</span>
<span class="code-keyword">&lt;button</span> <span class="code-string">class="submit-button"</span><span class="code-keyword">&gt;</span>Click me<span class="code-keyword">&lt;/button&gt;</span>

<span class="code-comment">/* In some CSS file, far away */</span>
<span class="code-keyword">.submit-button</span> {
  background-color: blue; color: white;
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem; border-radius: 0.25rem;
}

<span class="code-comment">&lt;!-- With Tailwind: no CSS file needed --&gt;</span>
<span class="code-keyword">&lt;button</span> <span class="code-string">class="tw-bg-blue-500 tw-text-white tw-px-4 tw-py-2 tw-rounded"</span><span class="code-keyword">&gt;</span>
  Click me
<span class="code-keyword">&lt;/button&gt;</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p>Each class does exactly <span class="highlight-orange">one thing</span>. You read the class names and immediately know what the element looks like.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">03</span>
  <h2>The Pattern: Property-Value</h2>
  <p>Tailwind class names follow a readable pattern: <span class="highlight">property shorthand - value</span>. Once you learn the pattern, you can guess any class name.</p>
</section>

<div class="class-ref reveal">
  <div class="cr-box">
    <div class="cr-title">TAILWIND CLASS &#8594; CSS IT GENERATES</div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-flex</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">display: flex</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-flex-col</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">flex-direction: column</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-flex-row</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">flex-direction: row</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-gap-4</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">gap: 1rem</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-gap-8</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">gap: 2rem</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-px-4</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">padding-left: 1rem; padding-right: 1rem</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-py-2</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">padding-top: 0.5rem; padding-bottom: 0.5rem</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-mt-8</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">margin-top: 2rem</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-my-2</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-justify-between</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">justify-content: space-between</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-items-center</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">align-items: center</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-border-t-4</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">border-top-width: 4px</span></div>
    <div class="cr-row"><span class="cr-class">tw-text-xs</span><span class="cr-arrow">&#8594;</span><span class="cr-css">font-size: 0.75rem</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">04</span>
  <h2>The tw- Prefix — Why This Repo Is Different</h2>
  <p>Standard Tailwind classes have no prefix: <code style="font-size:0.875rem">flex</code>, <code style="font-size:0.875rem">gap-4</code>, <code style="font-size:0.875rem">px-4</code>.</p>
  <p>But in this repo every class has <code style="font-size:0.875rem">tw-</code> in front: <code style="font-size:0.875rem">tw-flex</code>, <code style="font-size:0.875rem">tw-gap-4</code>. Why?</p>
  <p>This repo uses <span class="highlight">both Tailwind and Vuetify</span> — two CSS frameworks at the same time. Without a prefix, their class names would clash and break each other's styles.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">// Without prefix — potential clash</span>
<span class="code-string">flex</span>   <span class="code-comment">← is this Tailwind or Vuetify?</span>

<span class="code-comment">// With tw- prefix — no ambiguity</span>
<span class="code-string">tw-flex</span>   <span class="code-comment">← clearly Tailwind</span>
<span class="code-string">v-flex</span>    <span class="code-comment">← clearly Vuetify</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p>This prefix is configured in <code style="font-size:0.875rem">tailwind.config.js</code>:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-keyword">export default</span> {
  <span class="code-string">prefix: 'tw-'</span>,   <span class="code-comment">← this one line adds tw- to every class</span>
  presets: [tailwindConfig],
  content: [<span class="code-string">'./src/**/*.vue'</span>, <span class="code-string">'./src/**/*.js'</span>],
}</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">05</span>
  <h2>All Classes Are Predefined</h2>
  <p>You never write the CSS behind Tailwind classes. Tailwind already wrote them. They live in <code style="font-size:0.875rem">node_modules/tailwindcss/</code>.</p>
  <p>You just pick from what already exists and apply the class name. No CSS file needed on your side.</p>
  <p>ACV has also added <span class="highlight-orange">custom classes</span> in the shared Vuejs package — brand-specific colours like <code style="font-size:0.875rem">tw-text-grey-dark</code> and <code style="font-size:0.875rem">tw-border-t-primary-dark</code>. These are ACV's own Tailwind extensions on top of the standard set.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">06</span>
  <h2>@apply — Using Tailwind Inside SCSS</h2>
  <p>Sometimes you cannot add classes directly to an HTML element — for example when styling the internals of a third-party component. Tailwind's <code style="font-size:0.875rem">@apply</code> lets you use utility classes inside SCSS files:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">/* Inside a .vue file's &lt;style&gt; section */</span>
<span class="code-keyword">.auction-log-notes</span> {
  ::v-deep <span class="code-keyword">.acv-cta-table__records</span> {
    <span class="code-keyword">@apply</span> <span class="code-string">tw-border-t-4 tw-border-t-primary-dark tw-mb-4</span>;
  }
}</pre>
  </div>

  <p>This applies three Tailwind utilities to a deeply nested element without touching the HTML. This is real code from this repo.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 7 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">07</span>
  <h2>Purging — How Production Stays Small</h2>
  <p>Tailwind has thousands of possible utility classes. If all of them were included in the final CSS file, it would be enormous. Instead, Tailwind <span class="highlight">purges</span> unused classes during production build:</p>

  <div class="flow reveal" style="max-width:44rem;margin:1.5rem auto">
    <div class="flow-box fb-purple">All Tailwind classes<br><small>~3MB of CSS</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&#10132;</div>
    <div class="flow-box fb-orange">Scan all .vue and .js files<br><small>find every tw- class used</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&#10132;</div>
    <div class="flow-box fb-green">Only used classes<br><small>~10KB of CSS</small></div>
  </div>

  <p>The <code style="font-size:0.875rem">content</code> field in tailwind.config.js tells Tailwind where to scan: <code style="font-size:0.875rem">./src/**/*.vue</code> and <code style="font-size:0.875rem">./src/**/*.js</code>. Every unused class is stripped. The final CSS file is tiny.</p>
</section>

<!-- RECAP -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">RECAP</span>
  <h2>What We Learned</h2>
  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">Tailwind solves four CSS problems</strong> — naming, ever-growing files, inconsistency, and constant file switching.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e3f2fd;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #0984e3">
      <strong style="color:#0984e3">All classes are predefined</strong> — Tailwind already wrote the CSS. You just pick class names and apply them in HTML. No CSS file needed.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fef9e7;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #f9ca24">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">This repo uses tw- prefix</strong> — because it uses both Tailwind and Vuetify. The prefix prevents class name conflicts. Configured in tailwind.config.js.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">Purging keeps production small</strong> — Tailwind scans your files, keeps only classes you actually used, removes everything else.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHECKPOINT QUIZ -->
<section class="section reveal quiz-ep17">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">CHECKPOINT</span>
  <h2>Probe Your Thinking</h2>
  <p class="quiz-subtitle">No pressure. Just see what stuck.</p>
  <div class="quiz-grid">

    <div class="quiz-card">
      <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 01</span></div>
      <p class="quiz-question">What does the Tailwind class tw-flex do?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Adds a flexible border around the element</button></li>
        <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Sets display: flex on the element</button></li>
        <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Makes the element flexible in width</button></li>
        <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Wraps child elements automatically</button></li>
      </ul>
      <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
      <div class="quiz-answer"><div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) Sets display: flex</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">tw-flex is a pre-written Tailwind class that applies display: flex to an element, making it a flex container. Each Tailwind class does exactly one CSS property.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="quiz-card">
      <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 02</span></div>
      <p class="quiz-question">Why does this repo use tw- prefix on all Tailwind classes?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>It's a Tailwind requirement for Vue projects</button></li>
        <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>It makes the code more readable</button></li>
        <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>To prevent class name conflicts between Tailwind and Vuetify</button></li>
        <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>To reduce the final CSS bundle size</button></li>
      </ul>
      <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
      <div class="quiz-answer"><div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) To prevent class name conflicts between Tailwind and Vuetify</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">This repo uses both Tailwind and Vuetify. Without a prefix their class names could clash. The tw- prefix is configured in tailwind.config.js and keeps the two frameworks completely separate.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="quiz-card">
      <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 03</span></div>
      <p class="quiz-question">What does "purging" mean in the context of Tailwind?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Removing unused JavaScript from the bundle</button></li>
        <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Scanning your code and removing Tailwind classes that were never used</button></li>
        <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Clearing the browser cache after deployment</button></li>
        <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Deleting node_modules before a fresh install</button></li>
      </ul>
      <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
      <div class="quiz-answer"><div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) Removing unused Tailwind classes from the final CSS</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Tailwind ships with thousands of utility classes. Purging scans your .vue and .js files during build, keeps only the classes you actually used, and removes everything else — shrinking the final CSS from ~3MB to ~10KB.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- NEXT -->
<section class="next-part" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#e17055,#0984e3)">
  <h2>Ready for Episode 19?</h2>
  <p>Tailwind keeps CSS clean. ESLint and Stylelint keep JavaScript and CSS correct. Episode 18 covers what linters are, how they work, and how this repo configures them.</p>
  <a href="part19.html" class="btn btn-white">Continue to Episode 18 &#10132;</a>
</section>
`;
---
<ArticleLayout title="How the Frontend Works — Ep 17: Tailwind CSS | Unbox the Black Box" inlineStyle={inlineStyle} series={frontendSeries} episodeNum={18} hasQuiz={true}>
<Fragment set:html={bodyHtml} />
</ArticleLayout>
